WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.240
We have a right circular cone with a shaded base.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.800
And we will try to derive a formula for the surface area of a cone.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.180
Let us name the points O, A, B.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.460
Points A and B are marked on the circumference

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:22.380
such that AB forms a diameter.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.040
Let 'l' be the slant height and 'h' be the perpendicular height.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:31.860
And let 'r' be the radius of the circular base.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.980
First, let us consider the circular base.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.060
The radius of the circular base is 'r'.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.240
So it's area will be 'pi r squared'.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.360
Next we need to find the area of the curved surface.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.620
And we have seen that when the curved surface is opened up,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.920
it looks like a sector.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.440
We also need to find the area of the sector.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.640
Let the sector be O, A, B.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.040
Look at the sector and the cone.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.740
What do you think is the measure of these two lengths?

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.100
'OA' and 'OB' will equal the slant height which is 'l'.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.760
Here's another interesting question.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.740
What will be the length of arc AB?

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.520
This is an arc as we know that a sector is a part of the circle.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.220
This arc is actually the circumference of the circular base.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.040
So the length of this arc will be '2 pi r' .

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.980
A sector is also defined by the angle it subtends at the center.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.720
Let us call it 'theta'.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.600
Now we are ready to find the area of the sector!

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.380
And it gets even more interesting.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.860
There is another way in which we can find the length of the arc 'AB'.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.240
Let's zoom out to look at the other way.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.340
It will equal 'theta by 360'

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:00.820
multiplied by the circumference of the circle with radius 'OA' and 'OB'.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.520
What will be the circumference of the circle

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.060
that is formed with this as the sector?

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.320
It will be '2 pi l',

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.960
since 'l' is the radius of the circle of which this sector is a part.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.660
This tells us that the length of the arc AB

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:21.600
is a part of the circumference of the big circle that can be formed here.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.780
But the length of arc AB is also '2 pi r'.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.000
That will equal 'theta by 360' multiplied by '2 pi l'.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.360
Cancelling '2 pi' out from both sides,

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:41.180
we get 'r' equal to 'theta by 360' multiplied by 'l'.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:46.760
The area of this sector will be the curved surface area of this cone.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:51.380
The area of this sector will equal 'theta by 360'

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:57.420
multiplied by the area of the circle with 'OA' and a 'OB' as the radius.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:03.520
And that will be 'pi l squared' as 'l' is the radius of this circle.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.220
Can we write this a bit differently?

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.860
Yes, this can also be written as 'pi l' multiplied by

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.700
'theta by 360' multiplied by 'l'.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.680
We have just split 'pi l squared', into 'pi l' multiplied by 'l'.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:25.260
But this part in brackets as which is found out is equal to 'r'.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:30.600
So the curved surface area will be 'pi l' multiplied by 'r'.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.980
Let us review what we just did.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.440
We found out the length of arc AB

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.580
in two different ways and equated them.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.700
And then we found out the curved surface area

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.140
using the formula of a sector of a circle.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.800
And then finally substituted the value of 'r' in this formula

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.680
to eliminate 'theta'.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.060
Now, it's very simple!

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.660
The total surface area of the cone

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.640
will be the curved surface area

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.240
plus the area of the circular base.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.120
The curved surface area as we know is 'pi l r'.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:10.340
And the area of the base is 'pi r squared'.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.860
Both the terms have 'pi r' as their common factor.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.560
Taking 'pi r' common, we write the formula as 'pi r'

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.280
multiplied by 'l plus r'.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.900
This is the formula for the total surface area of a right circular cone.

